Secure Shell protocol (SSH) is a method used to securely send commands to a computer over an unsecured network.
SSH sets up a connection between a device and, most commonly, a server. To connect to the server, SSH uses the TCP/IP protocol, which is 
what is used by most of the internet. This connection is protected by encryption that prevents eaves droppers from viewing the raw data. This encryption is done via 
public key cryptography. Public key cryptography is part of asymmetric cryptography, where each party has a private and public key that they use to encrypt/ decrypt data
to prove their identity as well as providing a secure connection. Unlike HTTPS, both user and server are authenticated during SSH. After both parties are authenticated,
they agree on a symmetric key to use for the rest of the data transfer. This is because symmetric encryption is less expensive computationally, and is quicker to use.

